              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR75, TR85</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year in-home</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR75H, TR95H</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not listed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR85H</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not listed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Read that table as two warranties. Below $1,000, XTERRA covers the frame for life and almost everything
            that actually fails for 90 days, so a console, belt or controller fault in month four is your bill. From
            the TR65 up, the motor is covered for life and parts for one or two years, which is closer to what
            Horizon and Sole give. Where XTERRA lists no motor term, as on the TRX1400, WalkSlim and Hiker models,
            treat the motor as a part and ask XTERRA in writing. XTERRA’s own warranty page describes its list as
            taken from the product pages and still to be confirmed, and its FAQ says the same of the TR65 example,
            so register the machine and keep the warranty card that comes in the box.
          </p>
          <p>
            We did not find an extended-warranty plan on XTERRA’s store. XTERRA’s process for a claim is to find
            the 16-character serial number on the frame near the power cord, call its US support line, and have a
            part shipped or a technician sent. What a part costs after cover ends is covered in our guides to <a href="/treadmill-motor-replacement-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill motor replacement costs</a> and <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranties</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="returns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What a Return Costs</h2>
<p>
            XTERRA’s return policy for orders bought directly from it is one of the more generous at this price, as
            long as you have not built the machine. These are the terms on its return policy page on 28 November
            2026:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Changed your mind, still boxed and unused:</strong> 60 days from delivery. You pay the
            return freight; no restocking fee.</li>
            <li><strong>Changed your mind after assembling and trying it:</strong> 30 days from delivery. You pay
            the return freight and a 10% restocking fee.</li>
            <li><strong>Arrived damaged, defective or wrong:</strong> 14 days from delivery; XTERRA pays freight
            both ways and replaces it.</li>
            <li><strong>Demo, floor-model or refurbished units:</strong> 14 days, exchange only.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The restocking fee on an assembled machine is $45 on a TRX1000 at $449.99, $50 on a TRX1200, $130 on a
            TR75 and $220 on a TR95H. XTERRA does not publish what return freight costs, and for a 216 lb Sport
            treadmill it can be the larger bill, so ask before you send one back. XTERRA’s FAQ describes the
            window more tightly, as a Return Authorization requested within 30 days of delivery and the machine
            returned in its original packaging within 10 days of it, and says that policy is still to be
            confirmed; keep the box until you are sure. Our <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a> guide compares other brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>XTERRA vs Horizon, Sole and NordicTrack at the Same Money</h2>
<p>
            XTERRA’s pitch is price, so the fair test is what the same money buys from the bigger brands. The
            table uses prices already on this site and the Amazon prices we saw on 28 November, each dated.
