        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where People Go Wrong When Pricing an XTERRA Treadmill</h2>
          <p>XTERRA’s prices are low. The mistakes come from where you buy and what the warranty leaves out.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming Amazon is always cheaper</h3>
              <p>On 28 November Amazon was $300 cheaper on the TR75 but $136 dearer on the TRX1200 and $200 dearer on the TR75H. Check both prices for the exact model on the same day.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the regular price</h3>
              <p>XTERRA showed the TRX1200 against $879.99 on 5 November and $699.99 on 28 November. Compare the sale price with the history on this page, not with the “save” figure.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the 90-day parts cover</h3>
              <p>The Compact, WalkSlim and TF models cover parts and labour for 90 days. If you will use the treadmill daily, the TR65 or TR75, with lifetime motor cover and one or two years on parts, may be the cheaper machine over five years.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not planning for curbside freight</h3>
              <p>The Sport and Hiker models arrive at the kerb by freight in a box of up to 240 lb, with no stairs and no assembly. Line up help before the delivery window, or pay someone local.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">An XTERRA treadmill costs $399.99 to $2,199.99, and most buyers pay $449.99 to $1,299.99.</strong> On 28 November 2026 XTERRA had the TRX1200 at $499.99, the TR75 at $1,299.99 and the TR95H Hiker at
            $2,199.99, with a Black Friday and Cyber Monday sale on its walking and Compact models and the TR75H.
            There is no membership, and Amazon had the TR75 at $999.98.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Buy by where the money goes.</strong> For walking and light jogging, the
            TRX1200 from XTERRA is the price to beat, as long as 90 days of parts cover does not worry you. For
            running, the TR75 at $999.98 on Amazon is the XTERRA worth buying; at $1,299.99 a Sole F65 is the better
            deal. See our <a href="/xterra-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">XTERRA treadmill guide</a> before you order.
          </p>
